###
What is new since 0.2.5, one numbered block per feature.

Each block starts with a comment saying what to do and what you should see.
Where you are asked to type something, the line is not here yet: press Enter
at the end of the line marked with an arrow and type it yourself. Undo
afterwards and the file is back to clean.

Open the whole sample-project folder, not just this file, so the language
server indexes library.sk and the others alongside it. TESTING.md lists the
Zed settings this needs; the short version is semantic tokens on, and
"show_signature_help_after_edits": true for argument hints after a completion.

Exactly one line in this file is wrong on purpose: the misspelt option in
block 4. It should be the only diagnostic you see.
###

options:
	prefix: &8[&6Bank&8]&r
	farewell: &7See you soon,
	docs: https://docs.skriptlang.org/docs.html

# ------------------------------------------------------- 1. variable completion
#
# Completion inside braces offers the names your scripts actually use, since
# Skript declares almost nothing up front.

# Locals are scoped to their trigger. On the marked line, press Enter and type
# `{_` — you should be offered _balance, _p and _amount (the parameters count),
# and not _visitor, which belongs to the `on join:` further down.
function giveCoins(p: player, amount: number = 10):
	set {_balance} to {coins::%{_p}'s uuid%} ? 0
	add {_amount} to {_balance}
	set {coins::%{_p}'s uuid%} to {_balance} # <- press Enter here

# Globals come from the whole project. List variables complete one level at a
# time: on the marked line, press Enter and type `{data::` — the choices are
# `%player's uuid%`, `settings` and `*`. Pick `settings` and the next level
# offers motd, volume and `*`. A plain `{` lists every global in every file,
# including the ones in showcase.sk and library.sk.
on join:
	set {_visitor} to player
	add 1 to {data::%player's uuid%::joins}
	set {data::settings::motd} to "Welcome back!"
	set {data::settings::volume} to 0.5
	send {data::settings::motd} to {_visitor} # <- press Enter here

on quit:
	delete {data::%player's uuid%::*}

# ---------------------------------------------------------- 2. colour swatches
#
# Every colour code below carries a small swatch of the colour it produces:
# legacy `&6`, Spigot's `&x&f&f&5&5&5&5` hex, MiniMessage names like `<red>`
# and MiniMessage hex like `<#55ffff>`. The `prefix` option at the top has
# them too. Click a swatch and pick a different colour: it is written back in
# the same style it had, so a legacy `&` code comes back as `&x&…` legacy hex
# and a `<…>` tag comes back as `<#rrggbb>`. `&l` and `&r` are formatting, not
# colours, and get no swatch.
on first join:
	send "&6Gold, &x&f&f&5&5&5&5legacy hex, <red>a named colour, <#55ffff>MiniMessage hex" to player
	send "&a&lBold green&r and <dark_purple>dark purple" to player

# ----------------------------------------------------------- 3. clickable links
#
# Ctrl+click (Cmd+click on macOS) a URL to open it: inside a <link:…> tag,
# bare in a string, in an option value (the `docs` option above), or in a
# comment like this one: https://github.com/sovdeeth/oopsk
on respawn:
	send "<link:https://docs.skriptlang.org/>Click to open the Skript docs" to player
	send "Docs are also at {@docs}" to player
	send "Report bugs at https://github.com/SkriptLang/Skript/issues" to player

# ------------------------------------------------------ 4. unknown option + fix
#
# DELIBERATE ERROR. The broadcast below uses `farewel`, but the options block
# declares `farewell`. It is reported as an unknown option even though it sits
# inside a string. Put the cursor on it and open code actions (Ctrl+. or
# Cmd+.): the quick fix adds `farewel:` to the `options:` block, which clears
# the error. Undo that afterwards so the file keeps its one example.
on quit:
	broadcast "{@farewel} %player%!"

# -------------------------------------------------------------- 5. signature help
#
# On the marked line, press Enter and type `reward(`. The hint lists all four
# parameters, and the default `vector(1, 7, 0)` stays one piece instead of
# being split at its commas. Type `player, ` and `amount` is highlighted; then
# type `loud: ` and `loud` is highlighted, even though it is the third
# argument written, because a named argument fills the parameter it names.
#
# If the hint vanishes after you accept a completion, Zed needs
# "show_signature_help_after_edits": true.
function reward(p: player, amount: number = 10, offset: vector = vector(1, 7, 0), loud: boolean = true):
	add {_amount} to {coins::%{_p}'s uuid%}
	drop a gold nugget at {_p}'s location ~ {_offset}
	if {_loud} is true:
		send "{@prefix} &a+%{_amount}% coins" to {_p}

on join:
	reward(p: player, loud: false) # <- press Enter here

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 6. hover
#
# Hover each of these on the lines below:
#   {_p}          the variable, and where it is set
#   helmet        in `{_p}'s helmet`: Armor Slot, not the Set effect around it
#   floor         Skript's built-in floor() function and its documentation
#   reward        your own function's signature, from block 5
#   giveCoins     the same, from block 1
on respawn:
	set {_p} to player
	set {_hat} to {_p}'s helmet
	set {_amount} to floor(7.8)
	reward({_p}, {_amount})
	giveCoins({_p})
	send "%{_hat}%" to {_p}

# ------------------------------------------------------- 7. Skript 2.17 subcommands
#
# `subcommand` lines used to be parse errors that broke the outline and folding
# for the whole file. Open the outline (Ctrl+Shift+O): /bank contains
# `subcommand balance`, the `subcommands` group, and `subcommand give …` inside
# it. Each one folds on its own. Arguments named in a subcommand pattern become
# locals of its trigger, so hover `{_target}` below.
command /bank:
	description: Check your coins, or give some away.
	executable by: players
	trigger:
		send "{@prefix} Try /bank balance or /bank give." to player
	subcommand balance:
		trigger:
			send "{@prefix} You have %{coins::%player's uuid%} ? 0% coins." to player
	subcommands:
		subcommand give <target: player> <amount: number>:
			trigger:
				reward(p: {_target}, amount: {_amount})
				send "{@prefix} Sent %{_amount}% coins to %{_target}%." to player

# ------------------------------------------------------------- 8. oopsk structs
#
# https://github.com/sovdeeth/oopsk
#
# `struct Message:` is a template, not an event. It shows in the outline with
# its three fields and folds. `const` makes a field read-only after creation;
# hover `timestamp` to see its type and default. The `converts to:` section
# says how a Message becomes a string, which is why the broadcast below can
# print one directly.
#
# With oopsk detected (the generated test server has it, see README.md), hover
# `struct copy` and `template of` in the trigger for oopsk's own documentation.
struct Message:
	contents: string
	sender: offline player
	const timestamp: date = now
	converts to:
		string via this->contents

on chat:
	set {_message} to a message struct instance:
		sender: player
		contents: message
	set {_copy} to a struct copy of {_message}
	broadcast {_copy}
	set {_kind} to template of {_message}
	send "That was a %{_kind}% struct." to player
